The vectors are drawn on a square grid: $\overrightarrow{OA}=\mathbf{a}$, $\overrightarrow{OB}=\mathbf{b}$, and $\overrightarrow{OC}=\mathbf{c}$.

![Vector diagram](images/a7/a7c8651a67d96846172792412818a0b3dca3b473ec064abbfc6a5ddc304be19e.png)

Given that $\mathbf{c}=\lambda\mathbf{a}+\mu\mathbf{b}$, find $\lambda$ and $\mu$.

$\lambda=$ {{field-1}}

$\mu=$ {{field-2}}

question.:question/worked-solution:
![Completed vector route](images/16/16e49d3f1ffc9798f206c2fd6e956589f884075bb984fb9a5cf8aa8a8f5addb5.png)

Construct the route $O\to X\to C$, first parallel to $\mathbf{b}$ and then parallel to $\mathbf{a}$.

Along $OX$, move $2$ squares left. A copy of $\mathbf{b}$ moves $1$ square left. Thus $OX=2|\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{OX}=2\mathbf{b}$.

Along $XC$, move $2$ squares left and $2$ squares down. A copy of $\mathbf{a}$ moves $1$ square left and $1$ square down. Thus $XC=2|\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{XC}=2\mathbf{a}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{c}&=\overrightarrow{OX}+\overrightarrow{XC}\\&=2\mathbf{b}+2\mathbf{a}\\&=2\mathbf{a}+2\mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}
$$

Reorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\lambda=2$ and $\mu=2$.

